---
import MainLayout from '../../layouts/MainLayout.astro';

const errorShape = `{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Validation failed: Client Number cannot be blank",
  "errors": [
    "Client Number cannot be blank",
    "Amount must be greater than the discount"
  ],
  "fieldErrors": {
    "client_number": "Client Number cannot be blank"
  }
}`;

const handler = `async function save(objectApiName, body) {
  const res = await fetch(\`/api/v1/records/\${objectApiName}\`, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'X-API-Key': key },
    body: JSON.stringify(body),
  });

  const payload = await res.json();

  if (res.ok) return payload.data;

  // fieldErrors is keyed by field API name — attach each one to its input.
  if (res.status === 400 && payload.fieldErrors) {
    return { fieldErrors: payload.fieldErrors, formErrors: payload.errors ?? [] };
  }

  // Everything else is a single message worth surfacing verbatim.
  throw new Error(payload.message || \`Request failed (\${res.status})\`);
}`;

const ruleError = `{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Client Number cannot be blank",
  "errors": ["Client Number cannot be blank"]
}`;
---

<MainLayout
  title="Errors"
  description="The error envelope, validation errors, and what to do about each status code."
>
  <h1>Errors</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Failures use the same envelope as successes, with
    <code>success: false</code>. Anything non-2xx has a
    <code>message</code> you can show a user.
  </p>

  <h2>The error envelope</h2>
  <pre><code>{errorShape}</code></pre>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Always present</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>message</code></td>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>A single human-readable summary. Safe to display.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>errors</code></td>
          <td>No — validation failures only</td>
          <td>Every problem found, as a flat list. Good for a form-level summary.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>fieldErrors</code></td>
          <td>No — validation failures only</td>
          <td>Keyed by field API name, for highlighting individual inputs.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>Validation reports everything at once.</strong> All field errors and
    all triggered validation rules are collected before the response is sent, so
    a user fixes one round of problems rather than discovering them one at a time.
  </div>

  <h2>Status codes</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>400</code></td>
          <td>Field validation failed, a validation rule was triggered, or the body was malformed.</td>
          <td>Show <code>fieldErrors</code> against inputs. Do not retry unchanged.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>401</code></td>
          <td>No valid credential, or the key is revoked or expired.</td>
          <td>Re-authenticate. Don't retry with the same credential.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>403</code></td>
          <td>Authenticated, but a role permission, key scope or IP allow-list denies it.</td>
          <td>A configuration problem, not a transient one. Surface it to an admin.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>404</code></td>
          <td>No such object or record <em>in this tenant</em>.</td>
          <td>Check the <code>apiName</code> and the active tenant.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>409</code></td>
          <td>Uniqueness conflict — a unique field value or a taken <code>apiName</code>.</td>
          <td>Change the conflicting value.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>429</code></td>
          <td>Rate limit tripped.</td>
          <td>Honour <code>Retry-After</code> and back off. See <a href="/api/rate-limits">Rate Limits</a>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>500</code></td>
          <td>Unhandled server error.</td>
          <td>Safe to retry once with backoff. Report it if it persists.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="warning">
    <strong>Only <code>429</code> and <code>500</code> are worth retrying.</strong> Every
    other <code>4xx</code> will fail identically until the request or the
    configuration changes.
  </div>

  <h2>Validation rule failures</h2>
  <p>
    Object validation rules are cross-field checks that run on every save. A rule
    <strong>blocks the save when its expression evaluates to true</strong> — the
    expression describes the condition to reject. Its configured error message is
    returned verbatim:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{ruleError}</code></pre>
  <p>
    These arrive as <code>400</code>s alongside field errors. Because a rule is
    about the record as a whole, it is normally reported at form level rather than
    against a single input — though a rule may name a field. See
    <a href="/concepts/validation-rules">Validation Rules</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>A broken rule surfaces as a validation error, not a crash.</strong> If a
    rule's expression fails to parse or throws (division by zero, say), the save is
    rejected with a message naming the rule — it does not 500.
  </div>

  <h2>Handling errors</h2>
  <pre><code>{handler}</code></pre>

  <h2>Development versus production</h2>
  <p>
    Outside <code>NODE_ENV=development</code>, <code>500</code> responses omit stack
    traces and internal detail. Don't parse <code>message</code> to detect a
    specific failure — branch on the status code, which is stable, rather than on
    wording, which is not.
  </p>
</MainLayout>
